#. +> trunk5
#: ../../user_manual/ekos-mcp.rst:3
msgid "AI Assistant (MCP)"
msgstr ""

#. +> trunk5
#: ../../user_manual/ekos-mcp.rst:8
msgid "Introduction"
msgstr ""

#. +> trunk5
#: ../../user_manual/ekos-mcp.rst:10
msgid "Ekos embeds a Model Context Protocol (MCP) server. MCP is an open standard that lets AI assistants and agents discover and call tools exposed by an application. With the MCP server enabled, an AI assistant can observe your rig — connection status, mount position, the last captured image — and, if you allow it, operate the mount and focuser, resolve catalog targets, and more."
msgstr ""

#. +> trunk5
#: ../../user_manual/ekos-mcp.rst:12
msgid "Any MCP-capable client can consume the server out of the box: it speaks standard JSON-RPC 2.0 over HTTP, with the usual ``initialize``, ``tools/list`` and ``tools/call`` methods. No KStars-specific client code is required."
msgstr ""

#. +> trunk5
#: ../../user_manual/ekos-mcp.rst:21
msgid "Enabling the Server"
msgstr ""

#. +> trunk5
#: ../../user_manual/ekos-mcp.rst:23
msgid "The server is configured in the Ekos settings dialog, in the :guilabel:`MCP` tab."
msgstr ""

#. +> trunk5
#: ../../user_manual/ekos-mcp.rst:25
msgid "Check :guilabel:`Enable MCP server` to start it. The server starts automatically with Ekos whenever this option is enabled."
msgstr ""

#. +> trunk5
#: ../../user_manual/ekos-mcp.rst:27
msgid ":guilabel:`Port` selects the TCP port to listen on. The default is 8765; any port between 1024 and 65535 may be used."
msgstr ""

#. +> trunk5
#: ../../user_manual/ekos-mcp.rst:29
msgid "The status LED next to the port shows whether the server is currently listening."
msgstr ""

#. +> trunk5
#: ../../user_manual/ekos-mcp.rst:33
msgid "The server only listens on the local machine (127.0.0.1). It is not reachable from the network. To use it from another machine, you need to set up your own tunnel (for example an SSH port forward) and understand the security implications of doing so."
msgstr ""

#. +> trunk5
#: ../../user_manual/ekos-mcp.rst:38
msgid "Authentication and Tokens"
msgstr ""

#. +> trunk5
#: ../../user_manual/ekos-mcp.rst:40
msgid "Every request must carry a bearer token. Two tokens are managed in the :guilabel:`MCP` tab, each with show, copy and :guilabel:`Regenerate` buttons:"
msgstr ""

#. +> trunk5
#: ../../user_manual/ekos-mcp.rst:42
msgid "The :guilabel:`Token` field holds the full-access token. Clients presenting it can call every enabled tool."
msgstr ""

#. +> trunk5
#: ../../user_manual/ekos-mcp.rst:44
msgid "The :guilabel:`Read-only token` field holds a separate token restricted to read-only tools. Give this one to assistants that should observe the rig but never move hardware."
msgstr ""

#. +> trunk5
#: ../../user_manual/ekos-mcp.rst:46
msgid "Tokens are stored in your system keychain (under the service name ``kstars``), never in plain-text configuration files. Regenerating a token immediately invalidates the previous one."
msgstr ""

#. +> trunk5
#: ../../user_manual/ekos-mcp.rst:48
msgid "To connect a client, point it at ``http://127.0.0.1:8765/mcp`` and pass the token as a standard HTTP bearer credential. Requests are JSON-RPC 2.0 messages sent with ``POST``. For example, calling the ``ekos_status`` tool with curl:"
msgstr ""

#. +> trunk5
#: ../../user_manual/ekos-mcp.rst:50
msgid ""
"curl -s http://127.0.0.1:8765/mcp \\\n  -H \"Authorization: Bearer <your token>\" \\\n  -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n  -d '{\"jsonrpc\": \"2.0\", \"id\": 1, \"method\": \"tools/call\",\n"
"       \"params\": {\"name\": \"ekos_status\", \"arguments\": {}}}'"
msgstr ""

#. +> trunk5
#: ../../user_manual/ekos-mcp.rst:58
msgid "The response is a JSON-RPC envelope whose ``result`` carries the tool output — here the INDI connection state, the active profile, and the connected devices."
msgstr ""

#. +> trunk5
#: ../../user_manual/ekos-mcp.rst:60
msgid "A Server-Sent Events stream is available at ``/mcp/stream``. Each connection is rate-limited to 60 requests."
msgstr ""

#. +> trunk5
#: ../../user_manual/ekos-mcp.rst:65
msgid "Safety Controls"
msgstr ""

#. +> trunk5
#: ../../user_manual/ekos-mcp.rst:69
msgid "A full-access token allows an AI agent to move your mount and focuser. On a live rig, treat that token like a password: prefer the read-only token whenever observation is enough, and review which tools are enabled before handing control to an agent."
msgstr ""

#. +> trunk5
#: ../../user_manual/ekos-mcp.rst:71
msgid "Three mechanisms limit what a connected client can do:"
msgstr ""

#. +> trunk5
#: ../../user_manual/ekos-mcp.rst:73
msgid ":guilabel:`Read-only mode (refuse rig control tools)`. When checked, the server refuses every mutating tool for all clients, regardless of which token they present."
msgstr ""

#. +> trunk5
#: ../../user_manual/ekos-mcp.rst:75
msgid "The read-only token. Clients authenticating with it can only call tools marked read-only, even when read-only mode is off."
msgstr ""

#. +> trunk5
#: ../../user_manual/ekos-mcp.rst:77
msgid "The :guilabel:`Available tools` tree. Every tool the server exposes is listed here, grouped by family, with a checkbox per tool and per family. Unchecked tools are hidden from clients entirely: they disappear from ``tools/list`` and calls to them are rejected."
msgstr ""

#. +> trunk5
#: ../../user_manual/ekos-mcp.rst:83
msgid "In the tree, a lock icon marks read-only tools and a gear icon marks tools that change the state of the rig. Tooltips also indicate tools whose effect is hard to undo (such as ``mount_sync``), following the annotation hints of the MCP specification."
msgstr ""

